<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">UBS</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1998">1998</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as UBS Group</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">UBS is a Swiss bank formed in 1998 by the merger of the Union Bank of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> and the Swiss Bank Corporation, and one of the world's largest wealth managers. During the financial crisis of 2008 it needed a federal rescue, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> investigation of its role in tax evasion helped end Swiss bank secrecy towards foreign tax authorities. In 2023 it took over its rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/credit-suisse">Credit Suisse</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ubs.jpg" alt="UBS" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: JaierRT · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:UBS_HQ_Zurich.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>UBS has two main predecessors. The older, the Swiss Bank Corporation, began in 1854 when six private banking firms in Basel pooled their resources as the Bankverein, a consortium that acted as an underwriting syndicate for its members. In 1871 it joined the German Frankfurter Bankverein to form the joint-stock Basler Bankverein, which suspended its dividend until 1879 after heavy losses in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. Following mergers in 1885 and 1886 it became the Schweizerischer Bankverein, and in 1917 its English name was changed to Swiss Bank Corporation.</p><p>The Bank in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winterthur">Winterthur</a> was founded in 1862 with a share capital of CHF 5 million to finance industry. It profited from its railroad connections and warehouses during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a>, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> prices rose sharply. In 1912 it fused with the Toggenburger Bank, a savings and mortgage bank founded in 1863 with a branch network in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. The merged firm took the name Union Bank of Switzerland in 1921.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the mid-1990s Union Bank of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> came under fire from dissident shareholders who criticised its conservative management. Martin Ebner, through his investment trust BK Vision, became its largest shareholder and pressed for restructuring. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/credit-suisse">Credit Suisse</a> proposed a merger in 1996, which the board rejected unanimously, but Ebner led a shareholder revolt that replaced the chairman, Robert Studer, with Mathis Cabiallavetta, one of the architects of the merger with the Swiss Bank Corporation. The merger took place in 1998.</p><p>On 3 November 2000 UBS merged with the American brokerage Paine Webber, and in 2003 the name Paine Webber disappeared in favour of the single UBS brand. At the beginning of 2007 UBS was the first Wall Street firm to announce a heavy loss in the subprime mortgage sector, and during the financial crisis in 2008 GIC Private Limited invested CHF 11 billion in it. In March 2023 UBS agreed to buy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/credit-suisse">Credit Suisse</a> in an emergency rescue deal, which it completed on 12 June 2023.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>UBS is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joint-stock-company">joint-stock company</a> under Swiss law, with its headquarters in both Zurich and Basel. Its business covers wealth management, asset management, investment banking and banking for private and corporate clients in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. The bank draws on Swiss traditions of privacy. Banking secrecy goes back to the 1700s, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swiss-banking-act-of-1934">Federal Act on Banks and Savings Banks</a> of 1934 criminalised giving client information to third parties. Its secrecy practices have led to disputes with foreign tax authorities, and UBS reached agreements with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> tax service in 2009 and with U.K. HM Revenue and Customs in 2010.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “UBS” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
